{
  "dom_id": "step-q348190",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{2}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{2}≈$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=1,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(1)$ and ${f}^{′}(1)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(1)=\\sqrt{1}=1,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{2}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & ≈L(2) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(2-1) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
